<|end|>
<|asst|>
Of course! Here's an example of how you can extract multiple values from the same nested object in a JSON object using C# code:
```csharp
using System;
using Newtonsoft.Json.Linq;

class Program
{
 static void Main(string[] args)
 {
 // Example JSON object
 string json = @"
 {
 ""name"": ""John Smith"",
 ""age"": 30,
 ""address"": {
 ""street"": ""123 Main St"",
 ""city"": ""New York"",
 ""state"": ""NY"",
 ""zip"": ""10001""
 },
 ""phoneNumbers"": [
 {
 ""type"": ""home"",
 ""number"": ""555-1234""
 },
 {
 ""type"": ""work"",
 ""number"": ""555-5678""
 }
 ]
 }";
 // Parse the JSON object
 JObject obj = JObject.Parse(json);
 // Use LINQ to query the JSON object and extract the values of the "city" and "state" fields
 string city = (string)obj.SelectToken("address.city");
 string state = (string)obj.SelectToken("address.state");
 // Print the results
 Console.WriteLine(city);
 Console.WriteLine(state);
 }
}
```
In this example, we perform similar steps as before by parsing the JSON object into a JObject and querying it using LINQ. However, <|fim_suf|>, we print the results to the console. <|endoftext|>
