How can I extract the musical attributes of a jazz piece, specifically for the Miles Davis piece "So What," using Ruby code? 
Here is the code that can be used:
require 'rspotify'
client_id = 'your_client_id'
client_secret = 'your_client_secret'
RSpotify.authenticate(client_id, client_secret)
track = RSpotify::Track.search('So What', limit: 1, market: 'US').first
puts "Name: #{track.name}"
puts "Artist: #{track.artists.first.name}"
puts "Album: #{track.album.name}"
puts "Tempo: #{track.audio_features.tempo}"
puts "Key: #{track.audio_features.key}"
puts "Time Signature: #{track.audio_features.time_signature}" 
Make sure to replace "your_client_id" and "your_client_secret" with your own Spotify API credentials.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Unfortunately, the code you provided will not work for extracting musical attributes of a jazz piece, specifically for the Miles Davis piece "So What." This is because the code is using the Spotify API, which only provides audio features for songs that are available on Spotify.
However, you can use a different approach to extract musical attributes of "So What" by analyzing the audio file itself. One popular Ruby library for audio analysis is called "Essentia." Here is an example code snippet that uses Essentia to extract some musical attributes of "So What":
```
require 'rubygems'
require 'bundler/setup'
require 'essentia'
# Load the audio file
loader = Essentia::AudioLoader.new('path/to/so_what.mp3')
audio = loader.load_mono
# Extract some musical attributes
frame_size = 2048
hop_size = 1024
mfcc = Essentia::Extractor.new(:<|fim_suf|>, frame_size: frame_size, hop_size: hop_size).compute(audio)
spectral_centroid = Essentia::Extractor.new(:spectral_centroid, frame_size: frame_size, hop_size: hop_size).compute(audio)
spectral_flux = Essentia::Extractor.new(:spectral_flux, frame_size: frame_size, hop_size: hop_size).compute(audio)
# Print the extracted attributes
puts "MFCC: #{mfcc}"
puts "Spectral Centroid: #{spectral_centroid}"
puts "Spectral Flux: #{spectral_flux}"
```
Make sure to replace "path/to/so_what.mp3" with the actual path to your "So What" audio file. This code extracts the Mel-Frequency Cepstral Coefficients (MFCC), Spectral Centroid, and Spectral Flux of the audio file. You can modify the code to extract other musical attributes as well. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
Can Essentia be used to analyze live audio streams in real-time?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Yes, Essentia can be used to analyze live audio streams in real-time. Essentia provides a streaming mode that allows you to process audio samples in blocks as they arrive. Here is an example code snippet that shows how to use Essentia to compute the MFCC of a live audio stream:
```
require 'rubygems'
require 'bundler/setup'
require 'essentia'
# Create a streaming extractor
frame_size = 2048
hop_size = 1024
extractor = Essentia::StreamingExtractor.new(:mfcc, frame_size: frame_size, hop_size: hop_size)
# Start the stream
stream = Essentia::AudioLoader.new('default', sample_rate: 44100, channels: 1)
stream.enable_streaming
stream.start
# Process the stream in chunks
while true do
 chunk = stream.process_chunk
 break unless chunk
 features = extractor.process(chunk)
 puts "MFCC: #{features}"
end
```
This code creates a streaming extractor for the MFCC feature, then starts a live audio stream and processes it in chunks using the extractor. The extracted features are printed as they arrive. You can modify this code to extract other musical attributes in real-time as well. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


