<|user|>
What is the difference between a strongly typed language and a weakly typed language? Can you provide examples of each?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
A strongly typed language is one in which variables are bound to specific data types, and type checking is performed at compile time. In contrast, a weakly typed language is one in which variables can be bound to any data type, and type checking is performed at runtime. 

Some examples of strongly typed languages include Java, C++, and Swift. These languages require that variables be declared with specific data types, such as int or string, and will generate an error if the wrong type of data is assigned to a variable.

Examples of weakly typed languages include JavaScript, PHP, and Python. In these languages, variables are not bound to specific data types and can change their type during runtime. For example, a variable may start out as a number and then be reassigned as a string later on.

The difference between strongly and weakly typed languages has important implications for code safety and reliability. Strong typing can catch errors early on in the development process, while weak typing allows for more flexibility but can lead to unexpected behavior if variables are not properly initialized or type checked. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


