<|user|>
Show how the given inequality can be graphed
x + 2 > 5
<|end|>
<|asst|>
To graph the inequality x + 2 > 5, we need to first rewrite it in slope-intercept form by isolating the variable x:

x + 2 > 5 

x > 5 - 2 

x > 3 

This inequality can be read as "x is greater than 3." To graph this inequality on the number line, we start by drawing a circle at the point 3, which indicates that 3 is not included in the solution set. Then, we shade the region to the right of the circle to indicate that all values of x greater than 3 are solutions.

The graph would look like this:

<|fim_mid|><=======o---------------->

-2 0 1 2 3 4 5 6 ...

Note that the circle is an open circle instead of a closed circle<|fim_eom|>

