<|user|>
How do viruses differ from bacteria both structurally and functionally?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Viruses and bacteria differ in several ways. Structurally, viruses are much smaller than bacteria and consist only of genetic material (DNA or RNA) surrounded by a protein coat. They do not have their own metabolism and must rely on host cells to reproduce. Bacteria, on the other hand, are unicellular organisms with a variety of shapes and sizes, and they have their own metabolism and can reproduce independently. Functionally, viruses are obligate intracellular parasites that infect specific host cells, while bacteria can live and reproduce in a variety of environments. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
What is the difference between a scientific theory and a scientific law?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
A scientific theory is an explanation for a set of phenomena that has been extensively tested and supported by evidence, and which explains why the phenomena occur. It is subject to revision or rejection based on new evidence or data. A scientific law, on the other hand, is a concise statement that describes a fundamental relationship or pattern in nature that is universally observed. It does not explain why the phenomena occur, but rather describes what happens under certain conditions. Laws are also subject to revision or rejection based on new evidence, but they tend to be more stable and long-lasting than theories. <|endoftext|>
