<|fim_suf|>, meaning that any change to the input should result in a different hash. Hash functions are often used in security applications, such as password storage and digital signatures, because they can be used to verify that the original data has not been tampered with.

There are many different hash functions available, each with different properties and use cases. Some common hash functions include MD5, SHA-1, and SHA-256. These are all cryptographic hash functions, which means they are designed to be very difficult to reverse engineer or find collisions (i.e., two inputs that produce the same hash).

