Sure! A convolutional neural network, or CNN for short, is a type of neural network that is commonly used in computer vision tasks such as image classification, object detection, and segmentation. The main idea behind a CNN is to use convolutional layers to automatically learn features from the input data, such as images or videos, without requiring manual feature engineering. 

The basic building blocks of a CNN are convolutional layers, pooling layers, and activation functions. Convolutional layers apply a set of filters or kernels to the input data, which essentially act as feature detectors. Pooling layers reduce the spatial dimensionality of the output of the convolutional layer, typically by taking the maximum or average value in each local region. Activation functions introduce nonlinearity into the network and help it learn complex relationships between the input and output.

CNNs have achieved state-of-the-art performance on many computer vision benchmarks and are widely used in industry and academia. Would you like me to explain anything else about CNNs? <|endoftext|>
