<|fim_suf|>, insertion sort, and selection sort have a worst-case time complexity of O(n^2), while quick sort, merge sort, and heap sort have a worst-case time complexity of O(n log n). However, quick sort has the best average-case time complexity of O(n log n) among these algorithms.
Bubble sort is a simple algorithm that repeatedly swaps adjacent elements if they are in the wrong order. Insertion sort is similar to how people sort playing cards in their hand, where they repeatedly insert an element into its correct position in a sorted sublist. Selection sort repeatedly selects the smallest element from the unsorted portion of the list and swaps it with the first unsorted element. Quick sort, on the other hand, selects a pivot element and partitions the list into two sublists such that all elements in one sublist are less than or equal to the pivot and all elements in the other sublist are greater than the pivot. It then recursively sorts the two sublists. Merge sort works by dividing the list into two halves, recursively sorting each half, and merging the two sorted halves. Finally, heap sort builds a max heap from the list and repeatedly extracts the maximum element from the heap until the heap is empty. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


