Explain the concept of Mutual Exclusivity
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Mutual exclusivity is a principle that describes a relationship between two or more events that cannot occur simultaneously. In other words, if one event happens, the others cannot happen at the same time. This principle is often used in probability theory, statistics, and logic.

For example, in a coin toss, the outcome can either be heads or tails. These two outcomes are mutually exclusive because only one of them can occur at a time. Similarly, in a game of rock-paper-scissors, <|fim_mid|>the three possible outcomes (rock<|fim_eom|>

