<|user|>
Can you explain the concept of judicial review?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Judicial review is the power of the courts to interpret the meaning of laws and the Constitution and to declare them unconstitutional if they violate constitutional principles. This power was established by the landmark case Marbury v. Madison (1803), in which the Supreme Court asserted its authority to strike down unconstitutional laws passed by Congress. Judicial review is a critical component of the checks and balances system and ensures that no one branch of government becomes too powerful. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
What is the difference between civil rights and civil liberties?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Civil liberties are individual freedoms protected by law, such as freedom of speech, religion, and the press. Civil rights, on the other hand, are the protections and privileges granted to all citizens by law, regardless of race, gender, or other characteristics. Examples of civil rights include the right to vote, the right to a fair trial, and the right to equal treatment under the law. While civil liberties protect individual freedoms, civil rights ensure that everyone has equal access to those freedoms. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


