3. The 14th Amendment to the US Constitution granted citizenship to all persons born or naturalized in the United States. (True/False)
4. The 16th Amendment to the US Constitution established the federal income tax. (True/False)
5. The 18th Amendment to the US Constitution prohibited the manufacture, sale, or transportation of intoxicating liquors. (True/False)
6. The 19th Amendment to the US Constitution granted women the right to vote. (True/False)
7. The 20th Amendment to the US Constitution changed the date of the presidential inauguration from March 4th to January 20th. (True/False)
8. The 21st Amendment to the US Constitution repealed the 18th Amendment and ended prohibition. (True/False)
9. The 23rd Amendment to the US Constitution granted residents of Washington D.C. the right to vote in presidential elections. (True/False)
10. The 27th Amendment to the US Constitution prohibits Congress from giving itself a pay raise during the current session. (True/False) <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
What is the significance of the Second Amendment to the United States Constitution?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The Second Amendment to the US Constitution protects the right of citizens to bear arms. This amendment has been the subject of much controversy and debate, with some arguing that it guarantees an individual's right to own firearms for self-defense, while others argue that its primary purpose was to ensure that citizens could form militias to protect themselves against a tyrannical government. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
How has the interpretation of the First Amendment changed over time?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The First Amendment to the US Constitution guarantees freedoms of religion, speech, press, assembly, and petition. Over time, the interpretation of these freedoms has evolved through landmark court cases. For example, in Schenck v. United States (1919), the Supreme Court established the "clear and present danger" test, <|fim_mid|>which limits free speech if it poses a clear and present danger to national security or public safety. In Tinker v. Des Moines Independent Community School District (1969)<|fim_eom|>

