<|user|>
During a technical assessment, you are tasked with developing a robust Java program that can handle a large array of integers and accurately calculate the sum of all prime numbers contained within. In order to achieve this, you must leverage [Java code] data type to ensure optimal performance. Can you provide an optimized solution that efficiently identifies prime numbers within the array and returns the sum of those primes? As a hint, you may want to consider utilizing a helper method to accurately determine if a given number is prime or not. To ensure your solution is properly optimized, it must be able to handle inputs of varying sizes and compositions. Here is a sample input and output for your reference:
Input: [11, 14, 17, 19, 22, 24, 27, 29, 31, 33, 35, 37, 39]
Output: 97
Your solution should only consider prime numbers within the array, which in this case are 11, 17, 19, 29, 31, and 37.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Certainly! Here's an optimized solution in Java that uses a helper method to determine if a given number is prime or not:
```java
public class PrimeSumCalculator {
 
 public static void main(String[] args) {
 int[] arr = {11, 14, 17, 19, 22, 24, 27, 29, 31, 33, 35, 37, 39};
 int sum = sumOfPrimes(arr);
 System.out.println(sum);
 }
 
 public static int sumOfPrimes(int[] arr) {
 int sum = 0;
 for (int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
 if (isPrime(arr[i])) {
 sum += arr[i];
 }
 }
 return sum;
 }
 
 public static boolean isPrime(int n) {
 if (n <= 1) {
 return false;
 }
 for (int i = 2; i <= Math.sqrt(n); i++) {
 if (n % i == 0) {
 return false;
 }
 }
 return true;
 }
}
```
The `sumOfPrimes` method iterates through the input array and uses the `isPrime` helper method to determine if each number is prime or not. If a number is prime, it is added to the `sum` variable. The `isPrime` method uses a simple algorithm to check if a number is prime or not - it returns `false` if the number is less than or equal to 1, and iterates from 2 to the square root of the number checking if it is divisible by any number in that range. If it is, the method returns `false`, otherwise it returns `true`.
This solution should be able to handle inputs of varying sizes and compositions efficiently. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
That's a great solution! Can you explain what the time and space complexity of this program is?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Sure! The time complexity of the `sumOfPrimes` method is O(n*√n), <|fim_suf|>, we iterate from 2 to the square root of that number to check if it is prime or not. The space complexity of this program is O(1), as we are only using a fixed amount of memory to store the sum variable and the loop indices. Overall, this solution should be able to handle inputs of varying sizes and compositions efficiently while still accurately identifying prime numbers within the array and returning their sum. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


