<|user|>
How can we determine if a quadrilateral is cyclic or not?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
A quadrilateral is said to be cyclic if and only if the sum of opposite angles is equal to 180 degrees. In other words, if we draw a circle that passes through all four vertices of the quadrilateral, then it is a cyclic quadrilateral. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
What is the difference between a regular polygon and an irregular polygon?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
A regular polygon is a polygon in which all sides have equal length and all angles have equal measure, while an irregular polygon is a polygon that does not satisfy these conditions. Regular polygons are typically easier to work with as they exhibit more symmetry and have well-defined properties. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
Can you explain what the Pythagorean Theorem is?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The Pythagorean Theorem states that in a right triangle, the square of the length of the hypotenuse (the longest side) is equal to the sum of the squares of the lengths of the other two sides. In mathematical notation, if a and b are the lengths of the legs of a right triangle, and c is the length of the hypotenuse, then a^2 + b^2 = c^2. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
How do you find the area of a regular hexagon given its side length?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
To find the area of a regular hexagon with side length s, we can divide it into six congruent equilateral triangles. The height of each of these triangles is sqrt(3)/2 times the length of one side, <|fim_mid|>so the area of one of these triangles is (sqrt(3)/4)*s^2. Therefore<|fim_eom|>

