<|user|>
How do vaccines work to prevent diseases?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Vaccines work by stimulating the body's immune system to produce a response to a specific disease-causing organism or pathogen, without causing illness. Vaccines contain weakened or dead pathogens, parts of pathogens, or synthetic molecules that resemble pathogens, which trigger an immune response that produces antibodies to fight off future infections. 
These antibodies remain in the body and provide immunity to the specific pathogen, preventing future infection or reducing the severity of the disease if contracted. Vaccinations have been instrumental in preventing and eradicating many infectious diseases, including smallpox, polio, measles, and more recently, COVID-19. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


