What are the different types of clouds and how are they formed?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
There are several types of clouds, including cumulus, stratus, cirrus, and nimbus clouds, each with unique characteristics and formation methods. 
Cumulus clouds are puffy and white, commonly seen on sunny days, and form due to rising air currents carrying vapor upward. Stratus clouds are flat and often cover large portions of the sky, forming from horizontal layers of air. 
Cirrus clouds are thin and wispy, and generally found at high altitudes, formed from ice crystals in the atmosphere. Nimbus clouds are dark and often associated with rain or thunderstorms. 
Cloud formation is influenced by factors such as temperature, humidity, <|fim_mid|>and pressure. As moist air rises<|fim_eom|>

