<|user|>
Can you explain the origins of the idiom "a needle in a haystack"? Is it specific to English, or do other languages have similar expressions?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The origin of the idiom "a needle in a haystack" is not entirely clear, but it is believed to have been in use since at least the 16th century. One theory is that the phrase was inspired by the Bible, specifically the parable of the lost sheep found in Luke 15:3-7. In this story, a shepherd leaves his flock of 99 sheep to search for one that has gone missing, likening the task to finding a needle in a haystack.

Similar idioms exist in many other languages. For example, in Russian, the equivalent expression is "иголку в стоге сена найти" (find a needle in a haystack), and in German, it is "die Nadel im Heuhaufen suchen" (search for the needle in the haystack). These idioms are used to convey the same idea as the English expression - that something is very difficult to find among a large number of things. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
Are there any other idioms or expressions in French that are similar to "une aiguille dans une botte de foin"?
<|end|>
