There are also several animal-based idioms in Asian languages. For instance, in Chinese, the idiom "狗拿耗子，多管闲事" (literally "a dog catching rats interferes in other matters") means that someone is meddling in affairs that do not concern them. In Hindi, the phrase "haathi ke daant khane ke aur, dikhane ke aur" (literally "an elephant's teeth are for eating and for show") is used to describe something that has dual purposes or uses. <|endoftext|>
