<|user|>
Can you tell me more about the Orphism style of painting that Robert Delaunay was known for?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Of course! Orphism was a short-lived movement in art that emerged in France around 1910, and it was named after Orpheus, the musician and poet from Greek mythology who was said to have the power to charm all living things with his music. The movement was characterized by its use of vibrant colors and geometric shapes to create abstract compositions that were meant to evoke a sense of rhythm and harmony. Robert Delaunay was one of the key figures of Orphism, and he used circles and bold, contrasting colors to create what he called "simultaneous contrasts" in his paintings. His work had a significant influence on the development of Cubism, which was another important movement in modern art. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


