<|user|>
Can you explain the difference between a stack and a queue in computer science?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Yes, in computer science, both stacks and queues are data structures used to store collections of elements. However, there is a fundamental difference between them when it comes to the order in which elements are accessed.

A stack is a LIFO (Last-In-First-Out) data structure, meaning that the last element added to the stack is the first one to be removed. Think of it as a pile of plates where you can only add or remove plates from the top. Stacks are commonly used for implementing function calls, undo operations, and parsing expressions.

On the other hand, a queue is a FIFO (First-In-First-Out) data structure, meaning that the first element added to the queue is the first one to be removed. Think of it as a line of people waiting for a roller coaster ride, <|fim_mid|>where the person who arrived first gets to ride the roller coaster first. Queues are commonly used for implementing task scheduling<|fim_eom|>

