<|user|>
What is the difference between a Roth IRA and a Traditional IRA?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The main difference between a Roth IRA and a traditional IRA lies in the way they are taxed. With a Roth IRA, you make contributions with after-tax dollars, meaning you pay taxes on the money before you contribute it. However, when you withdraw funds from a Roth IRA, those withdrawals are tax-free, as long as you meet certain qualifications.

With a traditional IRA, on the other hand, you make contributions with pre-tax dollars, meaning you get a deduction on your current year's tax return for the amount of your contribution. However, when you withdraw funds from a traditional IRA, those withdrawals are taxed as ordinary income at your current tax rate.

Another key difference is that there are no age restrictions on contributing to a Roth IRA, while traditional IRAs require that you stop making contributions by age 70 ½. Additionally, Roth IRAs allow for penalty-free withdrawals of contributions at any time, while traditional IRAs have a 10% penalty for withdrawing funds before age 59 ½. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>




