Can you explain the difference between a stack and a queue data structure?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Sure! A stack and a queue are both linear data structures, meaning that they store their elements sequentially. However, <|fim_suf|>, which means that the last element added to the stack will be the first one to be removed. Think of it like a stack of plates - you always take the top plate off first.

On the other hand, a queue is a First-In-First-Out (FIFO) data structure, which means that the first element added to the queue will be the first one to be removed. Think of it like a line of people waiting for a bus - the person who arrived at the front of the line first will board the bus first.

In terms of implementation, you can implement a stack using an array or a linked list, while a queue can also be implemented using those data structures, but there are also specialized implementations such as a circular queue or a priority queue.

