<|user|>
What is the difference between supervised and unsupervised learning?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Supervised learning is a type of machine learning where the algorithm learns from labeled data. The algorithm is trained on input-output pairs, meaning the algorithm is given examples with known inputs and outputs so it can learn to recognize patterns in the data. Unsupervised learning, on the other hand, involves training an algorithm using unlabeled data. The goal of unsupervised learning is to find patterns or relationships in the data without being given any specific examples of what to look for. In other words, unsupervised learning involves finding structure in the data based solely on input data, without any pre-existing labels. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


