<|user|>
How can I generate a table of 10 fruits and their nutritional value using Excel? Here's a sample table format:
| Fruit | Calories (kcal) | Carbohydrates (g) | Protein (g) | Fat (g) | Fiber (g) |
|------------|----------------|-------------------|-------------|---------|-----------|
| Apple | 52 | 14 | 0.3 | 0.2 | 2.4 |
| Banana | 89 | 23 | 1.1 | 0.3 | 2.6 |
| Orange | 47 | 12 | 0.9 | 0.1 | 2.4 |
| Grapefruit | 52 | 13 | 0.8 | 0.2 | 2.0 |
| Mango | 135 | 35 | 1.1 | 0.6 | 3.7 |
| Pineapple | 50 | 13 | 0.5 | 0.1 | 1.4 |
| Kiwi | 61 | 15 | 1.1 | 0.5 | 2.1 |
| Blueberry | 57 | 14 | 0.7 | 0.3 | 2.4 |
| Strawberry | 33 | 8 | 0.7 | 0.3 | 2.0 |
| Raspberry | 52 | 12 | 1.2 | 0.7 | 6.5 |
Please provide the Excel formula/code to randomly select 10 fruits from a given list of fruits and populate their nutritional value in the above table format.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
To randomly select 10 fruits from a given list of fruits and populate their nutritional values in the above table format, you can follow these steps:
1. Create a list of fruits in a separate column or sheet. Let's say the list is in column A, starting from cell A2.
2. In the first row of the nutrition table (row 1), enter the headers for each column: Fruit, Calories (kcal), Carbohydrates (g), Protein (g), Fat (g), and Fiber (g).
3. In the "Fruit" column (column A), enter the following formula in cell A2: 
`=INDEX(A2:A11,RANDBETWEEN(1,10))` 
This formula will randomly select a fruit from the list in cells A2 to A11 (assuming there are 10 fruits in the list). You can adjust the range to match the number of fruits in your list.
4. In the "Calories (kcal)" column (column B), enter the following formula in cell B2: 
`=VLOOKUP(A2,A2:G11,2,FALSE)` 
This formula will look up the nutritional value for the selected fruit in the list and return the value in the second column (calories) of the same row.
5. Repeat step 4 for the remaining columns (Carbohydrates, Protein, Fat, and Fiber) by changing the column number in the formula. For example, in the "Carbohydrates (g)" column (column C), enter the following formula in cell C2: 
`=VLOOKUP(A2,A2:G11,3,FALSE)` 
6. Copy the formulas in cells A2 to F2 and paste them down to the remaining rows in the table.
Your table should now be populated with the nutritional values for 10 randomly selected fruits from your list. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
