<|user|>
What are some common types of conflicts found in literature?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Some common types of conflicts found in literature include man vs. man, man vs. self, man vs. nature, man vs. society, and man vs. technology. Man vs. man conflicts involve one character pitted against another, while man vs. self conflicts involve a character struggling with their own thoughts and emotions. Man vs. nature conflicts involve characters battling against natural elements or forces, while man vs. society conflicts involve characters going up against societal norms or institutions. Finally, man vs. technology conflicts involve characters struggling against machines or other forms of advanced technology. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
How do authors use resolution to bring closure to conflicts in literature?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Authors use resolution to bring closure to conflicts in literature by resolving the central conflict of the story and tying up loose ends. The resolution can provide a sense of closure for readers and give them a feeling of satisfaction that the story has been concluded. In some cases, the resolution may be a happy ending where all loose ends are tied up and the characters achieve their goals. In other cases, the resolution may be more ambiguous or unresolved, leaving readers to draw their own conclusions about what happened next. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


