<|user|>
Can you explain some of the lesser-known advantages of utilizing graph databases rather than traditional relational databases for data storage and analysis in a mathematical format? Please provide examples to support your explanation.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Certainly! Graph databases have several advantages over traditional relational databases, especially when it comes to data that has complex relationships between entities. 
One major advantage of graph databases is their ability to handle highly connected data. In a graph database, data is stored as nodes and edges, where nodes represent entities and edges represent the relationships between them. This makes it easy to model complex relationships between entities, such as social networks, recommendation engines, and fraud detection systems.
Mathematically, graph databases use graph theory to represent and analyze data. Graph theory is a branch of mathematics that deals with the study of graphs, which are mathematical structures used to model relationships between objects. In graph theory, <|fim_mid|>a graph is defined as a set of vertices (nodes) and a set of edges (connections between nodes).
One example of a graph database in action is the recommendation engine used by Netflix. Netflix uses a graph database to store information about users<|fim_eom|>

