Can you explain what polymorphism means in object-oriented programming?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Polymorphism is another important concept in object-oriented programming that refers to the ability of objects to take on multiple forms. In other words, an object of a particular class can be treated as an object of one of its parent classes or interfaces. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
How does polymorphism relate to inheritance?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Polymorphism can be achieved through inheritance. When a subclass inherits from a superclass, it can override or modify the behavior of any inherited methods. This means that a variable of the superclass type can refer to an object of either the superclass or any of its subclasses, and the appropriate method implementation will be called at runtime based on the actual class of the object. This is known as dynamic or runtime polymorphism. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


