<|fim_suf|>, so that it can accurately predict the output for new, unseen data. 

Unsupervised learning, on the other hand, involves training a model on unlabeled data, meaning that there are no predefined output variables. The goal of the model is to find patterns and structure within the input data without any guidance on what those structures should look like. This type of learning is often used for clustering or dimensionality reduction tasks.

An example of supervised learning would be training a model to recognize handwritten digits based on a dataset of labeled images. An example of unsupervised learning would be clustering similar customer demographics based on purchasing history data without any prior labels. <|endoftext|>
