Can you demonstrate how to construct a sentence in Go that features a subordinate clause serving as a nominal constituent and conveys a personal stance on a particular subject matter, while also ensuring the sentence adheres to the rules of grammatical agreement and avoids any potential ambiguities? Please share the relevant code that generates the sentence as well.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Sure, here's an example sentence in Go that meets your requirements:
```
package main
import "fmt"
func main() {
 subject := "the importance of education"
 verb := "is"
 clause := "something that cannot be overstated"
 personalStance := "I firmly believe"
 sentence := fmt.Sprintf("%s %s %s, %s.", personalStance, subject, verb, clause)
 fmt.Println(sentence)
}
```
This code will output the following sentence:
```
I firmly believe the importance of education is something that cannot be overstated.
```
In this sentence, <|fim_suf|>, modifying the noun phrase "the importance of education." The sentence also conveys a personal stance ("I firmly believe") and adheres to the rules of grammatical agreement. Additionally, it avoids any potential ambiguities by clearly indicating the subject ("the importance of education") and verb ("is"). <|endoftext|>
