<|user|>
Can you explain the concept of polymorphism in object-oriented programming and provide an example in Java?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Polymorphism is a key concept in object-oriented programming that refers to the ability of objects to take on multiple forms, depending on the context in which they are used. In Java, one way to achieve polymorphism is through inheritance and method overriding.

For example, let's say we have a class called "Shape" with a method called "draw." We can create subclasses of Shape, such as "Circle," "Square," and "Triangle," each with their own implementation of the "draw" method. When we call the "draw" method on an object of type "Shape," the appropriate implementation will be invoked based on the actual type of the object.

Here's some sample code that demonstrates this:

```
class Shape {
 public void draw() {
 System.out.println("Drawing a shape...");
 }
}

class Circle extends Shape {
 public void draw() {
 System.out.println("Drawing a circle...");
 }
}

class Square extends Shape {
 public void draw() {
 System.out.println("Drawing a square...");
 }
}

class Triangle extends Shape {
 public void draw() {
 System.out.println("Drawing a triangle...");
 }
}

public class Main {
 public static void main(String[] args) {
 Shape[] shapes = new Shape[3];
 shapes[0] = new Circle();
 shapes[1] = new Square();
 shapes[2] = new Triangle();

 for (Shape shape : shapes) {
 shape.draw();
 }
 }
}
```

In this example, we create an array of three Shape objects, with each element being an instance of a different subclass. When we loop through the array and call the "draw" method on each element, we get output that reflects the actual type of the object. This demonstrates the power of polymorphism in allowing us to write more flexible and modular code. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


