<|fim_suf|>, is the transfer of heat through a fluid medium such as air or water, due to the movement of the fluid caused by differences in temperature and density. For example, when you turn on a heater, warm air rises and cool air sinks, creating a convection current that circulates warm air throughout the room.

Radiation is the transfer of heat through electromagnetic waves, such as light or infrared radiation. It does not require a medium for heat to be transferred and can occur through a vacuum. An example of radiation is feeling the warmth of the sun on your skin.

In summary, conduction occurs through direct contact, convection occurs through the movement of a fluid medium, and radiation occurs through electromagnetic waves. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


