<|asst|>
Here is an example code snippet in C# that retrieves all products from the 'products' table, sorts them by price in ascending order, and stores the results in a list:
```csharp
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Data.SqlClient;
// Set up connection string
string connectionString = "Data Source=myServerAddress;Initial Catalog=myDataBase;User ID=myUsername;Password=myPassword";
// Set up SQL query to retrieve all products and sort by price
string query = "SELECT name, description, price, category FROM products ORDER BY price ASC";
// Create a list to store the results
List products = new List();
// Connect to the database and execute the query
using (SqlConnection connection = new SqlConnection(connectionString))
{
 SqlCommand command = new SqlCommand(query, connection);
 connection.Open();
 SqlDataReader reader = command.ExecuteReader();
 // Loop through the results and add each product to the list
 while (reader.Read())
 {
 Product product = new Product();
 product.Name = reader.GetString(0);
 product.Description = reader.GetString(1);
 product.Price = reader.GetDecimal(2);
 product.Category = reader.GetString(3);
 products.Add(product);
 }
 reader.Close();
}
// Define the Product class
public class Product
{
 public string Name { get; set; }
 public string Description { get; set; }
 public decimal Price { get; set; }
 public string Category { get; set; }
}
```
This code assumes that you have already installed the necessary SQL client library and that you have appropriate permissions to access the database. You may need to modify the connection string to match your specific database configuration. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
That code is helpful, but what if I want to only retrieve products with a price above a certain value?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
To retrieve products with a price above a certain value, you can modify the SQL query to include a WHERE clause that filters out any products with a price below the specified threshold. Here is an example code snippet in C# that retrieves all products from the 'products' table with a price greater than or equal to 50, sorts them by price in ascending order, and stores the results in a list:

```csharp
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Data.SqlClient;
// Set up connection string
string connectionString = "Data Source=myServerAddress;Initial Catalog=myDataBase;User ID=myUsername;Password=myPassword";
// Set up SQL query to retrieve products with a price greater than or equal to 50 and sort by price
string query = "SELECT name, description, price, category FROM products WHERE price >= 50 ORDER BY price ASC";
// Create a list to store the results
List products = new List();
// Connect to the database and execute the query
using (SqlConnection connection = new SqlConnection(connectionString))
{
 SqlCommand command = new SqlCommand(query, connection);
 connection.Open();
 SqlDataReader reader = command.ExecuteReader();
 // Loop through the results and add each product to the list
 while (reader.Read())
 {
 Product product = new Product();
 product.Name = reader.GetString(0);
 product.Description = reader.GetString(1);
 product.Price = reader.GetDecimal(2);
 product.Category = reader.GetString(3);
 products.Add(product);
 }
 reader.Close();
}
// Define the Product class
public class Product
{
 public string Name { get; set; }
 public string Description { get; set; }
 public decimal Price { get; set; }
 public string Category { get; set; }
}
```
You can modify the value 50 in the WHERE clause to any other price threshold you want. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


