<|user|>
How can I use JavaScript to find and present 3 quotes on the power of imagination? Can you provide a code snippet to extract and display these quotes from a given text source?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Yes, you can use JavaScript to find and present 3 quotes on the power of imagination. Here's an example code snippet that extracts and displays 3 quotes from a given text source:
```javascript
const textSource = `Imagination is more important than knowledge. Knowledge is limited. Imagination encircles the world. - Albert Einstein
Logic will get you from A to B. Imagination will take you everywhere. - Albert Einstein
Imagination is the beginning of creation. You imagine what you desire, you will what you imagine, and at last, you create what you will. - George Bernard Shaw
The power of imagination makes us infinite. - John Muir
Imagination is the highest kite one can fly. - Lauren Bacall`;
const quotes = textSource.match(/".*?"/g); // Extract all quotes from textSource
const randomQuotes = [];
// Get 3 random quotes from the extracted quotes
while (randomQuotes.length < 3 && quotes.length > 0) {
 const index = Math.floor(Math.random() * quotes.length);
 randomQuotes.push(quotes.splice(index, 1)[0]);
}
// Display the 3 random quotes
randomQuotes.forEach((quote, index) => {
 console.log(`Quote ${index + 1}: ${quote}`);
});
```
In this code snippet, <|fim_suf|>, we use a `while` loop to randomly select 3 quotes from the extracted quotes and store them in a `randomQuotes` array. Finally, we use a `forEach` loop to display the 3 random quotes in the console.
You can modify this code snippet to display the quotes in a different way, such as on a web page or in a pop-up window. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
That's great, thanks! Can you explain what a regular expression is and how it's used in JavaScript?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Sure! A regular expression (regex) is a sequence of characters that forms a search pattern. In JavaScript, regexes are used with string methods to perform powerful text processing and manipulation.

Regexes can be used to search, replace, and extract characters from strings based on specific patterns. For example, the `match` method we used in our code snippet accepts a regex as an argument and returns an array of all matches found within the string.

In JavaScript, regexes are defined using forward slashes (`/`). For example, `/hello/` is a regex that matches the string "hello". Regexes can also include special characters known as metacharacters, such as `.` (matches any character except line terminators), `*` (matches zero or more occurrences of the preceding element), and `[]` (matches any character within the brackets).

Regexes can be quite complex, but they are incredibly powerful tools for manipulating text data in JavaScript. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


