<|user|>
How does machine learning work, and what is the difference between supervised and unsupervised learning?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Machine learning is a type of artificial intelligence that enables computer systems to learn and improve from experience without being explicitly programmed. The learning process involves training a model on a dataset consisting of input data and corresponding output labels.

Supervised learning is a type of machine learning that involves training a model on a labeled dataset, where each input data point is accompanied by a corresponding output label. The goal is for the model to learn the underlying relationship between the input data and output labels, so that it can accurately predict the output label for new input data.

Unsupervised learning, on the other hand, involves training a model on an unlabeled dataset, where there are no corresponding output labels. The goal is for the model to identify patterns and relationships in the data without any prior knowledge, <|fim_mid|>so that it can group similar data points together or perform other types of unsupervised tasks.

