<|user|>
Can you explain what makes a number a prime number and how to determine if a number is prime?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
A prime number is a positive integer greater than 1 that has no positive integer divisors other than 1 and itself. To determine if a number is prime, you can start by checking if it is divisible by any of the primes less than or equal to the square root of that number. If none of these primes divide the number exactly, then the number is prime. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
Is there a way to quickly check if a large number is prime without checking all its factors?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Yes, there are several probabilistic primality tests that can quickly tell us whether a large number is likely to be prime or not. One such test is the Miller-Rabin primality test, <|fim_mid|>which uses modular arithmetic to test for primality. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
What is modular arithmetic and how is it used in the Miller-Rabin primality test?
