<|user|>
Create a new class called "AdventurousFriend" with the following properties:
- A string variable called "name"
- An integer variable called "age"
- A string variable called "breed"
- A boolean variable called "friendly"
- An array of strings called "territoriesExplored"
Create a constructor method that takes in parameters for all variables except "territoriesExplored" and initializes them accordingly.
Add a method called "explore" that randomly selects a territory from a pre-defined list and adds it to the "territoriesExplored" array.
Add a method called "greet" that prints out a greeting message that includes the animal's name and whether or not it is friendly.
Add a method called "getTerritoriesExplored" that returns the array of territories explored.
Now, modify the "explore" method to only add a new territory to "territoriesExplored" if it is not already in the array. 
Lastly, add a method called "getFriendlyStatus" that returns a boolean indicating whether or not the animal is friendly based on its age and breed. If the animal is younger than 2 years old and is a Golden Retriever, it should be considered friendly. Otherwise, it is not considered friendly.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
```
class AdventurousFriend {
 constructor(name, age, breed, friendly) {
 this.name = name;
 this.age = age;
 this.breed = breed;
 this.friendly = friendly;
 this.territoriesExplored = [];
 }
 explore() {
 const territories = ['Mountains', 'Beaches', 'Forests', 'Deserts', 'Islands'];
 const randomTerritory = territories[Math.floor(Math.random() * territories.length)];
 if (!this.territoriesExplored.includes(randomTerritory)) {
 this.territoriesExplored.push(randomTerritory);
 }
 }
 greet() {
 console.log(`Hello, my name is ${this.name} and I am ${this.friendly ? 'friendly' : 'not friendly'}.`);
 }
 getTerritoriesExplored() {
 return this.territoriesExplored;
 }
 getFriendlyStatus() {
 return this.age < 2 && this.breed === 'Golden Retriever';
 }
}
```
Example usage:
```
const dog = new AdventurousFriend('Buddy', 1, 'Golden Retriever', true);
dog.greet(); // Hello, my name is Buddy and I am friendly.
dog.explore();
console.log(dog.getTerritoriesExplored()); // ["Deserts"]
console.log(dog.getFriendlyStatus()); // true
``` <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
Can you explain polymorphism in JavaScript?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Polymorphism in JavaScript refers to the ability of different objects to respond to the same message or method call in different ways. This means that objects of different classes can have methods with the same name, but different implementations. For example, let's say we have two classes, `Rectangle` and `Circle`, both of which have a method called `getArea()`. 

When we call `getArea()` on an instance of `Rectangle`, it will calculate the area using the length and width properties of the rectangle. When we call `getArea()` on an instance of `Circle`, it will calculate the area using the radius property of the circle. This is an example of polymorphism because the `getArea()` method has different behavior depending on the class of the object that it is called on.

Polymorphism is one of the key principles of Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) and allows for more modular and extensible code. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>




