I love oatmeal raisin cookies, but I've always wondered why some recipes call for baking soda while others call for baking powder. What's the difference between the two?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Baking soda and baking powder are both leavening agents used to help baked goods rise. The difference is that baking soda needs an acid to activate it, while baking powder contains both the acid and the base needed for the reaction to occur.

When an acid (like vinegar or lemon juice) is added to baking soda, it creates carbon dioxide gas, which causes the dough or batter to rise. Baking powder already contains an acid (usually cream of tartar) and a base (usually baking soda), so it will react with the moisture in the recipe to produce carbon dioxide.

For recipes that contain an acidic ingredient (like buttermilk or yogurt), baking soda is usually used because it will be activated by the acid. For recipes that don't have an acidic ingredient, baking powder is the better choice because it already contains the necessary acid.

Did that explanation help clarify things for you? <|endoftext|>
<|end|>




