<|user|>
Why do some clouds produce rain while others do not?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Clouds produce rain when the water droplets inside them become too heavy to remain suspended in the air and fall to the ground. This happens when the air is saturated with water vapor and the cloud is unable to hold any more moisture. Some clouds, such as cirrus clouds, are composed of ice crystals and do not contain enough moisture to produce rain. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
How do hurricanes form?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Hurricanes form over warm ocean waters when a cluster of thunderstorms forms, producing a low-pressure center that draws in surrounding air. As the air rises and cools, it forms clouds and releases heat, which fuels the storm. The rotation of the Earth causes the storm to spin and gain strength, creating a hurricane. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


