Can you explain the concept of dark matter in astrophysics? What is it made of and how does it affect the universe?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Dark matter is a type of matter that is thought to exist in space, but it cannot be detected by any standard means. It doesn't interact with light or other forms of electromagnetic radiation, so it can't be seen directly. However, its existence can be inferred from its gravitational effects on visible matter, such as stars and galaxies.

The composition of dark matter is still unknown, but it is believed to be made up of particles that are different from the ones that make up ordinary matter. These particles don't emit, absorb or reflect light, which makes them extremely difficult to detect. Scientists have proposed several theories to explain the nature of dark matter, including WIMPs (Weakly Interacting Massive Particles) and axions, but there is currently no direct evidence to support any of these theories.

Despite its elusive nature, dark matter plays a crucial role in shaping the structure of the universe. Its gravity helps to hold galaxies together, and it affects the way that light bends and curves as it travels through space. In fact, dark matter is thought to account for around 85% of the matter in the universe, while ordinary matter makes up only about 15%. Without dark matter, the universe would look very different than it does today. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


