The male anglerfish has a specialized sense organ on its head that it uses to detect the scent of the female. Once he finds her, he bites onto her body and begins to fuse with her, eventually becoming nothing more than a pair of gonads attached to the female's body. The female then uses the sperm from the male whenever she needs to fertilize her eggs.

This extreme sexual dimorphism has evolved as a way for the male anglerfish to ensure that he will always have access to a mate, despite the fact that they live in deep, dark waters where finding a mate would otherwise be very difficult. However, it comes at a cost to the male, who gives up his independence and becomes entirely dependent on the female for survival.

