Here is an example of how you could use PHP code to accomplish this task:
```
php
 $json_data = '{"image_url": "https://example.com/image.jpg", "tweets": ["Tweet 1", "Tweet 2", "Tweet 3"], "hashtags": ["#food", "#yum", "#delicious"], "mentions": ["@chef", "@foodie", "@yum"]}';
 $data = json_decode($json_data, true);
 foreach($data["tweets"] as $key = $tweet) {
 $message = $tweet . " " . $data["hashtags"][$key] . " " . $data["mentions"][$key];
 $tweet_link = "https://twitter.com/intent/tweet?text=" . urlencode($message) . "&url=" . urlencode($data["image_url"]);
 echo "[Tweet $key]("$tweet_link")  
";
 }
?>
```
This code decodes a JSON object containing the image URL, an array of tweets, an array of hashtags, and an array of mentions. It then loops through each tweet and creates a new tweet for each one, including the corresponding hashtags and mentions, and outputs the tweet link to the console.
