<|user|>
How do giraffes survive in the wild?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Giraffes have many adaptations that help them survive in the wild. Their long necks allow them to reach leaves and branches high up in trees that other animals can't reach. They also have a specialized cardiovascular system that allows them to pump blood to their brains even when they lower their heads to drink water. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
Do giraffes live in groups or alone?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Giraffes typically live in loose groups of up to 20 individuals, although they may form larger herds in areas with abundant food. Within these herds, there is usually a dominant male who mates with the females and defends the group from predators. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
What kind of predators do giraffes face?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Giraffes are preyed upon by lions, <|fim_suf|>, leopards, and wild dogs. However, their large size and powerful kick make them difficult prey for most predators. In addition, giraffes have excellent eyesight and hearing, which allows them to detect danger from far away. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
How long do giraffes live for?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Giraffes typically live for around 25 years in the wild, although they can live longer in captivity. However, they face many threats to their survival, including habitat loss, poaching, and climate change. Conservation efforts are underway to protect giraffes and their habitat. <|endoftext|>
