<|user|>
Can you explain the difference between active and passive voice?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The active voice emphasizes the subject of the sentence as the doer of the action, while the passive voice emphasizes the object of the sentence as the receiver of the action. For example, in the active voice, <|fim_suf|>, while in the passive voice, "The apple was eaten by John," the apple is the object that received the action of being eaten. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
What are some common verb tenses in English?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Some common verb tenses in English include the present simple (e.g., "I eat"), present continuous (e.g., "I am eating"), past simple (e.g., "I ate"), past continuous (e.g., "I was eating"), present perfect (e.g., "I have eaten"), and past perfect (e.g., "I had eaten"). <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
Can you explain the concept of subject-verb agreement?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Subject-verb agreement refers to ensuring that the subject and verb in a sentence agree in number (i.e., singular or plural). For example, in the sentence "She walks to school," "she" is a singular subject, so the verb "walks" must also be singular. In the sentence "They walk to school," "they" is a plural subject, so the verb "walk" must be in its plural form. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
What are some common prepositions in English?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Some common prepositions in English include "in," "on," "at," "to," "from," "by," "with," and "for." Prepositions are used to show the relationship between two words in a sentence, such as the location of an object ("The book is on the table") or the manner in which something is done ("He ran with great speed"). <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


