What is the difference between an idiom and a proverb?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
An idiom is a phrase or expression whose meaning cannot be understood from the literal definition of its words. For example, "kick the bucket" means to die, but it has nothing to do with actual buckets or kicking. A proverb, on the other hand, is a short saying that expresses a commonly held truth or piece of advice, for example "A stitch in time saves nine." <|endoftext|>
