<|end|>
<|asst|>
The shebang, also known as the hashbang, is a special sequence of characters at the beginning of a script that specifies the interpreter to be used to execute the script. In a shell script, the shebang consists of the characters "#!" followed by the path to the desired shell interpreter. 
The purpose of the shebang is to allow the operating system to automatically determine the appropriate interpreter to use when executing the script. Without the shebang, the script would not be executable and would need to be manually executed using the appropriate interpreter, which can be inconvenient.
Furthermore, the shebang can also be used to specify command-line arguments to the interpreter, which can be useful in certain situations. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


