<|user|>
Thank you for the instructions. Can you explain how the radial-gradient function works in CSS? What are the parameters used to create the gradient effect? Are there any other shapes that can be used instead of a circle?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Sure! The `radial-gradient()` function in CSS creates a radial gradient background image. It starts from the center of an element and radiates outward, blending two or more colors together. Here's an example:

```
background: radial-gradient(red, yellow);
```

This will create a circle-shaped gradient that blends from red at the center to yellow at the edges.

You can also specify the size and shape of the gradient using the `at` keyword, followed by a position value and a shape value. For example:

```
background: radial-gradient(circle at center, red, yellow);
```

This will create a circle-shaped gradient that starts at the center of the element.

You can use other shapes as well, such as ellipse:

```
background: radial-gradient(ellipse at center, red, yellow);
```

Or even custom shapes using the `circle()` or `ellipse()` functions:

```
background: radial-gradient(circle(50px at 25% 25%), red, yellow);
```

This will create a circle-shaped gradient with a radius of 50 pixels, positioned at 25% from the left and top of the element.

I hope this helps! Let me know if you have any more questions. <|endoftext|>
