What is the difference between deductive and inductive reasoning?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Deductive reasoning is a form of logical reasoning where a conclusion is drawn from a set of premises or statements that are assumed to be true. In other words, if the premises are true, then the conclusion must also be true. Inductive reasoning, on the other hand, involves drawing general conclusions from specific observations or examples. Unlike deductive reasoning, the conclusion of an inductive argument is not guaranteed to be true, but rather it is based on probability and likelihood. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
How do vaccines work to protect us from diseases?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Vaccines work by introducing a weakened or inactive form of a virus or bacteria into the body, which triggers the immune system to produce an immune response. This response creates antibodies that recognize and attack the specific pathogen, and remember it for future encounters. If the person is exposed to the actual virus or bacteria later on, their immune system will be able to quickly recognize and destroy it before it has a chance to cause illness. This is how vaccines protect us from diseases. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>




