What is the difference between a virus and a bacterium?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Viruses and bacteria are both types of microorganisms, but they differ in several important ways. Bacteria are single-celled organisms that are capable of reproducing independently, while viruses are not cells and require a host cell in order to replicate. Bacteria can be found in a wide range of environments, including soil, water, and living organisms, and some species can cause disease in humans. Viruses are much smaller than bacteria and do not have the ability to metabolize or reproduce on their own. Instead, they hijack the machinery of host cells in order to produce more virus particles, which can lead to infection and disease. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>




